PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L12U3.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":12,"cefr":"C2","unit":3,"theme":"Literature and Society","essential_question":"How does literature reflect society, and can stories change the world?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1","title":"Its Pages Yellowed, Its Cover Torn: Absolute Constructions","objectives":["Students will be able to use absolute constructions to add descriptive detail in sophisticated writing.","Students will be able to distinguish absolute constructions (with their own subject) from dangling participles.","I can write \"Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.\"","I can write \"With the sun setting, we read poetry on the roof.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام التراكيب المطلقة لإضافة تفاصيل وصفية في الكتابة الرفيعة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من التمييز بين التراكيب المطلقة (التي لها فاعلها الخاص) والمعدِّلات المعلّقة.","أستطيع أن أكتب Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.","أستطيع أن أكتب With the sun setting, we read poetry on the roof."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"protagonist","pos":"noun","ipa":"/proʊˈtæɡənəst/","definition":"the main character in a story","arabic":"البطل / الشخصية الرئيسة","example":"The protagonist of the novel is a young teacher.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-W01"},{"word":"antagonist","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ænˈtæɡənəst/","definition":"the character who opposes the main character","arabic":"الخصم / الشخصية المضادة","example":"The antagonist tries to stop the hero.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-W02"},{"word":"narrator","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈnɛreɪtɚ/","definition":"the person who tells the story","arabic":"الراوي","example":"The narrator is the hero's younger sister.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-W03"},{"word":"setting","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɛtɪŋ/","definition":"the time and place in which a story happens","arabic":"الزمان والمكان / الإطار","example":"The setting is a village in 1950s Najd.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"An absolute construction is a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier. It is attached to the main clause with a comma and adds detail about circumstances, causes or accompanying actions. It has its own subject, different from the subject of the main clause: Her heart pounding, she opened the letter. (her heart = pounding; she = opened)\nPatterns: noun + -ing (Her hands shaking, he read the poem aloud.); noun + -ed (The work completed, the team went home. Its cover torn, the book…); noun + adjective / phrase / adverb (Pen in hand, the poet began to write. The meeting over, we left.). With with / without: With the sun setting, we read on the roof. With his eyes closed, he listened to the recitation.\nFixed absolutes: weather permitting, time permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal. Compare: Reading the novel, I cried. (participle – I was reading) vs The novel finished, I cried. (absolute – the novel was finished). An absolute avoids the dangling problem because it has its own subject: (not: Finished, I closed the novel) → The novel finished, I closed it.\nnoun + -ing / -ed + , + main clause (Her heart pounding, she…)\nnoun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …)\nwith / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …)\nfixed: weather permitting • all things considered • that being said","examples":["Its pages yellowed and its cover torn, the book had clearly been read many times.","Her heart pounding, she opened the publisher's letter.","The work completed, the writers celebrated.","Pen in hand, the poet waited for inspiration.","With the sun setting, we read poetry on the roof.","With his eyes closed, he listened to the recitation.","We will hold the book fair outside, weather permitting.","All things considered, it was an excellent first novel."],"common_errors":["✗ Her heart pounded, she opened the letter. → ✓ Her heart pounding, she opened the letter. (Comma splice → absolute with -ing.)","✗ The work was completed, the team went home. → ✓ The work completed, the team went home. (Absolute → no was.)","✗ Finished, I closed the novel. (The novel was finished.) → ✓ The novel finished, I closed it. (Give the absolute its own subject.)","✗ With the sun set, we began reading. (still in progress) → ✓ With the sun setting, we began reading. (-ing for an action in progress.)"],"arabic":"التركيب المطلق شبه جملة اسمية لها اسم فاعل أو اسم مفعول أو معدِّل خاص بها، وتلتحق بالجملة الرئيسة بفاصلة، ولها فاعلها الخاص: Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.\nالأنماط: اسم + -ing، واسم + -ed (The work completed)، واسم + صفة أو شبه جملة (Pen in hand / The meeting over)، وwith + اسم + -ing / -ed (With the sun setting).\nعبارات ثابتة: weather permitting (إن سمح الطقس)، all things considered (بالنظر إلى كل شيء)، that being said (ومع ذلك).\nالتركيب المطلق يتجنب مشكلة المعدِّل المعلّق لأن له فاعله الخاص."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Its Pages Yellowed, Its Cover Torn: Absolute Constructions”. Let’s learn absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U3-LS1 | Its Pages Yellowed, Its Cover Torn: Absolute Constructions","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “Why Stories Matter”: Its pages yellowed and its cover torn, the copy of Naguib Mahfouz's \"Palace Walk\" in my grandfather's library had clearly been read many times. Mahfouz, the first Arabic-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, described ordinary families in Cairo with such honesty that readers recognized their own lives in his characters. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"Its pages yellowed and its cover torn, the copy of Naguib Mahfouz's \"Palace Walk\" in my grandfather's library had clearly been read many times. Mahfouz, the first Arabic-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, described ordinary families in Cairo with such honesty that readers recognized their own lives in his characters.","visual":"Image GV-L12-U3-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"An absolute construction is a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier. It is attached to the main clause with a comma and adds detail about circumstances, causes or accompanying actions. It has its own subject, different from the subject of the main clause: Her heart pounding, she opened the letter. (her heart = pounding; she = opened) Patterns: noun + -ing (Her hands shaking, he read the poem aloud.); noun + -ed (The work completed, the team went home. Its cover torn, the book…); noun + adjective / phrase / adverb (Pen in hand, the poet began to write. The meeting over, we left.). With with / without: With the sun setting, we read on the roof. With his eyes closed, he listened to the recitation.","on_screen":"noun + -ing / -ed + , + main clause (Her heart pounding, she…) • noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …) • with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Its pages yellowed and its cover torn, the book had clearly been read many times. Her heart pounding, she opened the publisher's letter. The work completed, the writers celebrated.","on_screen":"Its pages yellowed and its cover torn, the book had clearly been read many times. / Her heart pounding, she opened the publisher's letter. / The work completed, the writers celebrated.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Her heart pounded, she opened the letter.” That’s not right. We say: “Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.” Comma splice → absolute with -ing.","on_screen":"✗ Her heart pounded, she opened the letter.   ✓ Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.\" What was pounding? … Think! … Her heart. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","التركيب المطلق شبه جملة اسمية لها اسم فاعل أو اسم مفعول أو معدِّل خاص بها، وتلتحق بالجملة الرئيسة بفاصلة، ولها فاعلها الخاص: Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Her heart pounded, she opened the letter. — الصحيح: Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Her heart pounding, she opened the letter."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Why Stories Matter","body":"Its pages yellowed and its cover torn, the copy of Naguib Mahfouz's \"Palace Walk\" in my grandfather's library had clearly been read many times. Mahfouz, the first Arabic-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, described ordinary families in Cairo with such honesty that readers recognized their own lives in his characters.\nLiterature has always held a mirror up to society. Written in the nineteenth century, Charles Dickens's novels exposed the harsh conditions in which poor children lived and worked in Britain, and they are often credited with encouraging social reform. George Orwell's \"Animal Farm,\" an allegory in which farm animals overthrow their human owner, warned readers about the dangers of absolute power.\nWriters from the Gulf have explored their own societies' transformations. In \"Cities of Salt,\" Abdul Rahman Munif portrays a desert community, its traditional way of life disrupted by the discovery of oil. More recently, the Saudi novelist Raja Alem, having grown up in Makkah, became the first woman to win the International Prize for Arabic Fiction, sharing the award in 2011 for \"The Dove's Necklace.\"\nWhy do such works matter? All things considered, stories do something statistics cannot: they allow us to live, for a few hours, inside another person's mind. With the world becoming increasingly divided, this ability to imagine other lives may be more important than ever. Some researchers have even suggested that reading literary fiction can strengthen empathy, although the evidence remains debated.\nThe last page turned, a good novel stays with us. Its questions follow us into our own lives, quietly changing the way we see the people around us.","word_count":268,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Her heart ___, she opened the letter from the publisher.","options":["pounds","was pounding","pounded","pounding"],"answer":"pounding","explanation":"Noun + -ing participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ing."},{"q":"The novel ___, the writer finally took a holiday.","options":["finishing it","was finished","finished","finishes"],"answer":"finished","explanation":"Noun + -ed participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ed."},{"q":"___ in hand, the poet waited for inspiration.","options":["Pen","Pens","The pen was","A pen is"],"answer":"Pen","explanation":"Noun + adjective / phrase / adverb.","explanation_ar":"اسم + صفة أو شبه جملة."},{"q":"With the sun ___, we read poetry on the roof.","options":["sets","setting","was setting","set it"],"answer":"setting","explanation":"with / without + noun + modifier.","explanation_ar":"with + اسم."},{"q":"We will hold the book fair in the garden, weather ___.","options":["permitting","to permit","permitted","permits"],"answer":"permitting","explanation":"Fixed absolute expressions.","explanation_ar":"العبارات الثابتة."},{"q":"______ the old storyteller began the tale that his grandfather had told him sixty years earlier.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["His voice trembled with emotion,","Trembling his voice with emotion","His voice trembling with emotion,","His voice was trembling with emotion,"],"answer":"His voice trembling with emotion,","explanation":"Noun + -ing participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ing."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Combine with an absolute construction.","items":[{"q":"The meeting was over.  +  The students left the library. (The meeting over, …)","options":[],"answer":"The meeting over, the students left the library.","explanation":"Noun + adjective / phrase / adverb.","explanation_ar":"اسم + صفة أو شبه جملة."},{"q":"His eyes were closed.  +  He listened to the recitation. (With his eyes closed, …)","options":[],"answer":"With his eyes closed, he listened to the recitation.","explanation":"with / without + noun + modifier.","explanation_ar":"with + اسم."},{"q":"The last chapter was written.  +  The author sent the manuscript to her publisher. (The last chapter written, …)","options":[],"answer":"The last chapter written, the author sent the manuscript to her publisher.","explanation":"Noun + -ed participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ed."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"Her hands shook, she read the poem aloud.","options":[],"answer":"Her hands shaking, she read the poem aloud.","explanation":"Her hands shook → Her hands shaking. Noun + -ing participle.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Her hands shook → Her hands shaking. اسم + -ing."},{"q":"The exams were over, we celebrated at the beach.","options":[],"answer":"The exams over, we celebrated at the beach.","explanation":"The exams were over → The exams over. Noun + adjective / phrase / adverb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: The exams were over → The exams over. اسم + صفة أو شبه جملة."},{"q":"Finished, I returned it to the library.","options":[],"answer":"The novel finished, I returned it to the library.","explanation":"Finished, I → The novel finished, I. Absolute vs dangling participle.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Finished, I → The novel finished, I. المطلق والمعلّق."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Describe with absolute constructions.","items":[{"q":"Describe a writer at work with \"noun + in hand\".","options":[],"answer":"Notebook in hand, my sister writes in the garden every morning.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a feeling with \"noun + -ing\".","options":[],"answer":"My hands shaking, I stood up to read my poem.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a finished task with \"noun + -ed\".","options":[],"answer":"The homework done, I picked up my favorite novel.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a scene with \"With + noun + -ing\".","options":[],"answer":"With the rain falling softly, we read stories by the window.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Use a fixed absolute.","options":[],"answer":"All things considered, the film was better than the book.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A descriptive paragraph.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences describing a character in a story with absolute constructions.","options":[],"answer":"Her eyes fixed on the horizon, the old woman waited for the ship. With the wind pulling at her scarf, she did not move.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence beginning \"That being said…\" after a criticism of a book.","options":[],"answer":"The ending was predictable. That being said, the characters were unforgettable.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence ending with an absolute construction.","options":[],"answer":"The children ran into the courtyard, their laughter echoing off the walls.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Four Corners Grammar","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["The teacher says a sentence frame with a cue word and four possible answers (A–D).","Students walk to the corner with the correct answer.","One student in each corner explains the choice in one sentence.","Correct corner scores a point; continue with the next cue."],"data":[{"label":"Its pages yellowed"},{"label":"The work completed"},{"label":"Her heart pounding"},{"label":"With the sun setting"},{"label":"Pen in hand"},{"label":"Having been translated"},{"label":"Written in 1945"},{"label":"When asked"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Her bags ___, she said goodbye to her family.","options":["packs","packed","packing them","were packed"],"answer":"packed","explanation":"Noun + -ed participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ed."},{"q":"The children ran into the room, their faces ___ with excitement.","options":["were glowing","glow","glowing","glowed"],"answer":"glowing","explanation":"Noun + -ing participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ing."},{"q":"The storm ___, the villagers came out of their houses.","options":["overing","is over","over","was over it"],"answer":"over","explanation":"Noun + adjective / phrase / adverb.","explanation_ar":"اسم + صفة أو شبه جملة."},{"q":"___ the lights off, the stage looked mysterious.","options":["Without of","Within","By","With"],"answer":"With","explanation":"with / without + noun + modifier.","explanation_ar":"with + اسم."},{"q":"___ considered, the novel is a remarkable achievement.","options":["All things are","All things","All thing","Everything is"],"answer":"All things","explanation":"Fixed absolute expressions.","explanation_ar":"العبارات الثابتة."},{"q":"","options":["The final scene writing, the playwright went to bed.","The final scene was written, the playwright went to bed.","The final scene written, the playwright went to bed.","Writing the final scene, the playwright was written."],"answer":"The final scene written, the playwright went to bed.","explanation":"Noun + -ed participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ed."},{"q":"","options":["With the audience watching in silence, the poet began.","With the audience was watching in silence, the poet began.","With the audience watched in silence, the poet began.","With watching the audience in silence, the poet began."],"answer":"With the audience watching in silence, the poet began.","explanation":"with / without + noun + modifier.","explanation_ar":"with + اسم."},{"q":"The novelist wrote the final chapter in a single night, ______ by the knowledge that the deadline was only hours away.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["she sharpened her concentration,","her concentration sharpen","her concentration was sharpened","her concentration sharpened"],"answer":"her concentration sharpened","explanation":"Noun + -ed participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ed."},{"q":"______ the library's oldest manuscripts were carefully moved to a new climate-controlled room.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["The renovation complete,","The renovation was complete,","Completing the renovation,","The renovation completing it,"],"answer":"The renovation complete,","explanation":"Noun + adjective / phrase / adverb.","explanation_ar":"اسم + صفة أو شبه جملة."},{"q":"In \"Her heart pounding, she opened the letter,\" what is the subject of \"pounding\"?","options":["her heart","the letter","nothing","she"],"answer":"her heart","explanation":"Absolute vs dangling participle.","explanation_ar":"المطلق والمعلّق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A grandfather's home library: an old copy of an Arabic novel with yellowed pages and a torn cover; a teenager reading on a rooftop at sunset with a cup of tea; shelves with books by Arab and world authors. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A grandfather's home library: an old copy of an Arabic novel with yellowed pages and a torn cover; a teenager reading on a rooftop at sunset with a cup of tea; shelves with books by Arab and world authors"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter)"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows protagonist (the main character in a story); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing protagonist: the main character in a story"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows antagonist (the character who opposes the main character); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing antagonist: the character who opposes the main character"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows narrator (the person who tells the story); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing narrator: the person who tells the story"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows setting (the time and place in which a story happens); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing setting: the time and place in which a story happens"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Four Corners Grammar”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Four Corners Grammar"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-W01","script":"protagonist. protagonist. The protagonist of the novel is a young teacher.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-W02","script":"antagonist. antagonist. The antagonist tries to stop the hero.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-W03","script":"narrator. narrator. The narrator is the hero's younger sister.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-W04","script":"setting. setting. The setting is a village in 1950s Najd.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-T1","script":"Its pages yellowed and its cover torn, the copy of Naguib Mahfouz's \"Palace Walk\" in my grandfather's library had clearly been read many times. Mahfouz, the first Arabic-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, described ordinary families in Cairo with such honesty that readers recognized their own lives in his characters. Literature has always held a mirror up to society. Written in the nineteenth century, Charles Dickens's novels exposed the harsh conditions in which poor children lived and worked in Britain, and they are often credited with encouraging social reform. George Orwell's \"Animal Farm,\" an allegory in which farm animals overthrow their human owner, warned readers about the dangers of absolute power. Writers from the Gulf have explored their own societies' transformations. In \"Cities of Salt,\" Abdul Rahman Munif portrays a desert community, its traditional way of life disrupted by the discovery of oil. More recently, the Saudi novelist Raja Alem, having grown up in Makkah, became the first woman to win the International Prize for Arabic Fiction, sharing the award in 2011 for \"The Dove's Necklace.\" Why do such works matter? All things considered, stories do something statistics cannot: they allow us to live, for a few hours, inside another person's mind. With the world becoming increasingly divided, this ability to imagine other lives may be more important than ever. Some researchers have even suggested that reading literary fiction can strengthen empathy, although the evidence remains debated. The last page turned, a good novel stays with us. Its questions follow us into our own lives, quietly changing the way we see the people around us.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Its Pages Yellowed, Its Cover Torn: Absolute Constructions”. Let’s learn absolute constructions (a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier, attached to a main clause): noun + -ing participle (Her heart pounding, she opened the letter); noun + -ed participle (The work completed, the team went home); noun + adjective / prepositional phrase / adverb (Its cover torn, …; Pen in hand, …; The meeting over, …); with / without + noun + participle or phrase (With the sun setting, …; With his eyes closed, …); fixed absolutes (weather permitting, all things considered, that being said, other things being equal); absolute vs dangling participle together. Listen to the start of “Why Stories Matter”: Its pages yellowed and its cover torn, the copy of Naguib Mahfouz's \"Palace Walk\" in my grandfather's library had clearly been read many times. Mahfouz, the first Arabic-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, described ordinary families in Cairo with such honesty that readers recognized their own lives in his characters. Look at the green words. An absolute construction is a noun phrase with its own participle or modifier. It is attached to the main clause with a comma and adds detail about circumstances, causes or accompanying actions. It has its own subject, different from the subject of the main clause: Her heart pounding, she opened the letter. (her heart = pounding; she = opened) Patterns: noun + -ing (Her hands shaking, he read the poem aloud.); noun + -ed (The work completed, the team went home. Its cover torn, the book…); noun + adjective / phrase / adverb (Pen in hand, the poet began to write. The meeting over, we left.). With with / without: With the sun setting, we read on the roof. With his eyes closed, he listened to the recitation. Here are some examples. Its pages yellowed and its cover torn, the book had clearly been read many times. Her heart pounding, she opened the publisher's letter. The work completed, the writers celebrated. Be careful! Some learners say: “Her heart pounded, she opened the letter.” That’s not right. We say: “Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.” Comma splice → absolute with -ing. Quick check: \"Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.\" What was pounding? … Think! … Her heart. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Her heart pounding, she opened the letter.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2","title":"Having Been Translated into Forty Languages…: Advanced Participle Clauses","objectives":["Students will be able to use perfect, passive and past participle clauses to combine ideas concisely.","Students will be able to use conjunctions with participles (when asked, once published) and avoid dangling participles.","I can write \"Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions of readers.\"","I can write \"When asked about her inspiration, the author smiled.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام جمل اسم الفاعل التام والمجهول واسم المفعول لدمج الأفكار بإيجاز.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام أدوات الربط مع اسم الفاعل والمفعول (when asked / once published) وتجنب المعدِّلات المعلّقة.","أستطيع أن أكتب Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions of readers.","أستطيع أن أكتب When asked about her inspiration, the author smiled."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"theme","pos":"noun","ipa":"/θim/","definition":"the main idea or message of a work of literature","arabic":"الموضوع / الفكرة الرئيسة","example":"The main theme of the novel is loyalty.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS2-W01"},{"word":"motif","pos":"noun","ipa":"/moʊˈtif/","definition":"an image or idea that is repeated in a work","arabic":"فكرة متكررة / موتيف","example":"Water is a recurring motif in the poem.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS2-W02"},{"word":"symbolism","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɪmbəˌlɪzəm/","definition":"the use of objects or images to represent ideas","arabic":"الرمزية","example":"The novel's symbolism is subtle but powerful.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS2-W03"},{"word":"metaphor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmɛtəfɔr/","definition":"a comparison that says one thing is another","arabic":"استعارة","example":"\"Time is a thief\" is a metaphor.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Perfect participle (Having + past participle) shows that one action happened before another: Having read the novel twice, she wrote a brilliant review. Perfect passive (Having been + past participle): Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions of readers. Past participle (passive meaning): Written in 1945, \"Animal Farm\" is still widely read.\nBeing + past participle gives a reason in the passive: Being written in dialect, the poems are difficult for some readers. Negative: put not first: Not having read the book, I could not answer. Not knowing the ending, we were surprised. Many conjunctions can come before a participle: While reading, she took notes. When asked about her inspiration, the author smiled. Once published, the book became a bestseller. If left unread, a book teaches nothing. Although written long ago, the story feels modern.\nThe implied subject of the participle must be the subject of the main clause. ✔ Having finished the novel, I returned it. ✘ (not: Having finished the novel, the library received it back.) (The library didn't finish it.) ✘ (not: When asked about the prize, the answer was modest.) → When asked about the prize, the author gave a modest answer.\nHaving + past participle (earlier action) • Having been + past participle (earlier, passive)\npast participle clause (Written in 1945, …) • Being + past participle (reason, passive)\nNot having… / Not knowing… (negative)\nwhen / while / once / if / although / until + participle • implied subject = main subject","examples":["Having read the novel twice, she wrote a brilliant review.","Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions.","Written in 1945, \"Animal Farm\" is still widely read.","Being written in dialect, the poems are hard for some readers.","Not having read the book, I couldn't join the discussion.","When asked about her inspiration, the author smiled.","Once published, the book became a bestseller.","Although written long ago, the story feels modern."],"common_errors":["✗ Having translated into many languages, the novel became famous. → ✓ Having been translated into many languages, the novel became famous. (The novel was translated → passive.)","✗ Having not read the book, I stayed silent. → ✓ Not having read the book, I stayed silent. (Not first.)","✗ When asked about the prize, her answer was modest. → ✓ When asked about the prize, she gave a modest answer. (Implied subject = main subject.)","✗ Writing in 1945, \"Animal Farm\" is still popular. → ✓ Written in 1945, \"Animal Farm\" is still popular. (The book was written → past participle.)"],"arabic":"اسم الفاعل التام (Having + التصريف الثالث) يدل على فعل سابق: Having read the novel twice, she wrote a review، والتام المجهول: Having been translated into forty languages, …\nاسم المفعول: Written in 1945, Animal Farm is still read، وBeing + التصريف الثالث للتعليل بالمجهول.\nالنفي بوضع not أولًا: Not having read the book…، وأدوات الربط قبل الاسم: When asked / Once published / Although written.\nالفاعل الضمني لاسم الفاعل يجب أن يكون فاعل الجملة الرئيسة، وإلا صار معلّقًا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Having Been Translated into Forty Languages…: Advanced Participle Clauses”. Let’s learn perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U3-LS2 | Having Been Translated into Forty Languages…: Advanced Participle Clauses","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: Once published, the poem was quoted everywhere. Not having been told about the change, the readers were confused. (Participle clauses are used in statements to combine ideas.)","on_screen":"Once published, the poem was quoted everywhere. | Not having been told about the change, the readers were confused. | (Participle clauses are used in statements to combine ideas.)","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Perfect participle (Having + past participle) shows that one action happened before another: Having read the novel twice, she wrote a brilliant review. Perfect passive (Having been + past participle): Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions of readers. Past participle (passive meaning): Written in 1945, \"Animal Farm\" is still widely read. Being + past participle gives a reason in the passive: Being written in dialect, the poems are difficult for some readers. Negative: put not first: Not having read the book, I could not answer. Not knowing the ending, we were surprised. Many conjunctions can come before a participle: While reading, she took notes. When asked about her inspiration, the author smiled. Once published, the book became a bestseller. If left unread, a book teaches nothing. Although written long ago, the story feels modern.","on_screen":"Having + past participle (earlier action) • Having been + past participle (earlier, passive) • past participle clause (Written in 1945, …) • Being + past participle (reason, passive) • Not having… / Not knowing… (negative)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Having read the novel twice, she wrote a brilliant review. Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions. Written in 1945, \"Animal Farm\" is still widely read.","on_screen":"Having read the novel twice, she wrote a brilliant review. / Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions. / Written in 1945, \"Animal Farm\" is still widely read.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Having translated into many languages, the novel became famous.” That’s not right. We say: “Having been translated into many languages, the novel became famous.” The novel was translated → passive.","on_screen":"✗ Having translated into many languages, the novel became famous.   ✓ Having been translated into many languages, the novel became famous.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Having been translated…\" Did the novel translate, or was it translated? … Think! … It was translated. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions of readers.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions of readers.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","اسم الفاعل التام (Having + التصريف الثالث) يدل على فعل سابق: Having read the novel twice, she wrote a review، والتام المجهول: Having been translated into forty languages, …","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Having translated into many languages, the novel became famous. — الصحيح: Having been translated into many languages, the novel became famous.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions of readers."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"___ the novel twice, she wrote a brilliant review.","options":["Having read","Being read","Having been read","Read"],"answer":"Having read","explanation":"Having + past participle (earlier action).","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"___ into forty languages, the novel has reached millions of readers.","options":["Having been translating","Having translated","Translating","Having been translated"],"answer":"Having been translated","explanation":"Having been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"Having been done."},{"q":"___ in 1945, \"Animal Farm\" is still widely studied in schools.","options":["Being writing","Written","Having written","Writing"],"answer":"Written","explanation":"Past participle clause (Written in…).","explanation_ar":"Written in…"},{"q":"___ the book, I couldn't join the class discussion.","options":["Not having been read","Having not read","Not having read","Having read not"],"answer":"Not having read","explanation":"Not having… / Not knowing…","explanation_ar":"النفي."},{"q":"When ___ about her inspiration, the author smiled and talked about her grandmother.","options":["having asked her","she asked","asked","asking"],"answer":"asked","explanation":"Conjunction + participle (When asked, Once published).","explanation_ar":"الأداة + اسم الفاعل."},{"q":"Although ______ more than fifty years ago, the novel's portrait of a family struggling with rapid change feels remarkably modern to today's readers.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["written","writing","having written","it written"],"answer":"written","explanation":"Conjunction + participle (When asked, Once published).","explanation_ar":"الأداة + اسم الفاعل."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Combine with a participle clause.","items":[{"q":"The poet finished her collection.  +  Then she sent it to three publishers. (Having…)","options":[],"answer":"Having finished her collection, the poet sent it to three publishers.","explanation":"Having + past participle (earlier action).","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"The book was published in 2011.  +  It won a major prize. (Published…)","options":[],"answer":"Published in 2011, the book won a major prize.","explanation":"Past participle clause (Written in…).","explanation_ar":"Written in…"},{"q":"The author was asked about her next novel.  +  She refused to give details. (When asked…)","options":[],"answer":"When asked about her next novel, the author refused to give details.","explanation":"Conjunction + participle (When asked, Once published).","explanation_ar":"الأداة + اسم الفاعل."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"Having translated into Arabic, the novel found new readers.","options":[],"answer":"Having been translated into Arabic, the novel found new readers.","explanation":"Having translated → Having been translated. Having been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Having translated → Having been translated. Having been done."},{"q":"Having not seen the film, I couldn't compare it with the book.","options":[],"answer":"Not having seen the film, I couldn't compare it with the book.","explanation":"Having not seen → Not having seen. Not having… / Not knowing…","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Having not seen → Not having seen. النفي."},{"q":"When asked about the prize, her answer was modest.","options":[],"answer":"When asked about the prize, she gave a modest answer.","explanation":"her answer was modest → she gave a modest answer. Implied subject = main subject.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: her answer was modest → she gave a modest answer. الفاعل الضمني."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Combine ideas concisely.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence beginning \"Having read…\".","options":[],"answer":"Having read all of Mahfouz's novels, my uncle now reads them again in English.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence beginning with a past participle about a book.","options":[],"answer":"Published in 1984, \"Cities of Salt\" describes the arrival of the oil industry.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence beginning \"Once…\" + past participle.","options":[],"answer":"Once finished, the mural will be the largest in the city.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence beginning \"Not having…\".","options":[],"answer":"Not having seen the play before, I didn't know the ending.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence beginning \"While + -ing\".","options":[],"answer":"While reading the poem, I noticed the repeated image of water.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A writer's biography.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences about a writer's life using participle clauses.","options":[],"answer":"Born in Makkah, Raja Alem grew up surrounded by stories. Having studied English literature, she began writing novels in Arabic.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"Having been + past participle\".","options":[],"answer":"Having been rejected by five publishers, the novel finally became a bestseller.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"Although + past participle\".","options":[],"answer":"Although written for children, the book is loved by adults.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Grammar Snakes & Ladders","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Roll and move. If you land on a cue square, make a sentence with the cue and perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause.","Correct at the bottom of a ladder: climb. Incorrect at the head of a snake: slide down.","Teammates are the judges and must explain any correction.","First player to reach square 30 wins."],"data":[{"cue":"the book / its pages / yellow (absolute)"},{"cue":"the novel / finish / the writer / rest (absolute)"},{"cue":"her heart / pound / she / open the letter"},{"cue":"the sun / set / we / read on the roof (with + noun + -ing)"},{"cue":"pen / in hand / the poet / begin"},{"cue":"the novel / translate / into 40 languages (Having been…)"},{"cue":"Animal Farm / write / in 1945 (past participle)"},{"cue":"the author / ask / about her inspiration (When asked…)"},{"cue":"not / read / the book / I / could not answer (Not having…)"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___ the manuscript, the editor called the author with good news.","options":["Read by","Having been read","Having read","Being read"],"answer":"Having read","explanation":"Having + past participle (earlier action).","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"___ by millions of readers, the poem became part of the national school curriculum.","options":["Been loved","Having been loved","Loving","Having loved"],"answer":"Having been loved","explanation":"Having been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"Having been done."},{"q":"___ in a remote village, the story is told by a young shepherd.","options":["Being setting","Set","Having set","Setting"],"answer":"Set","explanation":"Past participle clause (Written in…).","explanation_ar":"Written in…"},{"q":"___ the language, the tourists couldn't understand the play.","options":["Not knowing","Not known","Knowing not","Having not knew"],"answer":"Not knowing","explanation":"Not having… / Not knowing…","explanation_ar":"النفي."},{"q":"___ published, the novel was banned in several countries.","options":["Unless","Since","Until","Once"],"answer":"Once","explanation":"Conjunction + participle (When asked, Once published).","explanation_ar":"الأداة + اسم الفاعل."},{"q":"","options":["Having finished the book, I returned it to the library.","Having finished the book, it was returned to the library.","Having finished the book, the return was quick.","Having finished the book, the library received it back."],"answer":"Having finished the book, I returned it to the library.","explanation":"Implied subject = main subject.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل الضمني."},{"q":"","options":["Having writing in old Arabic, the poem needs a glossary.","Writing in old Arabic, the poem needs a glossary.","Being written in old Arabic, the poem needs a glossary.","Been written in old Arabic, the poem needs a glossary."],"answer":"Being written in old Arabic, the poem needs a glossary.","explanation":"Past participle clause (Written in…).","explanation_ar":"Written in…"},{"q":"______ for her first novel, the young writer was invited to speak at book festivals across the Arab world.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["Praising","Having praised","Having been praising","Having been praised"],"answer":"Having been praised","explanation":"Having been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"Having been done."},{"q":"If ______ properly, rare manuscripts can survive for many centuries, as the collections in the region's oldest libraries show.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["having stored them","storing","they stored","stored"],"answer":"stored","explanation":"Conjunction + participle (When asked, Once published).","explanation_ar":"الأداة + اسم الفاعل."},{"q":"Which sentence contains a dangling participle?","options":["The books looked ancient as I walked into the library.","Walking into the library, I noticed the ancient books.","Walking into the library, the books looked ancient.","When I walked into the library, the books looked ancient."],"answer":"Walking into the library, the books looked ancient.","explanation":"Implied subject = main subject.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل الضمني."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A book festival in Riyadh: an author signing books in front of a banner \"Translated into 40 languages\"; a journalist holding a microphone asking a question; a display of classic novels with dates of first publication. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A book festival in Riyadh: an author signing books in front of a banner \"Translated into 40 languages\"; a journalist holding a microphone asking a question; a display of classic novels with dates of first publication"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause: a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Literature and Society”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of perfect participle (Having read the novel, …)"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows theme (the main idea or message of a work of literature); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing theme: the main idea or message of a work of literature"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows motif (an image or idea that is repeated in a work); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing motif: an image or idea that is repeated in a work"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows symbolism (the use of objects or images to represent ideas); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing symbolism: the use of objects or images to represent ideas"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows metaphor (a comparison that says one thing is another); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing metaphor: a comparison that says one thing is another"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Grammar Snakes & Ladders”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Grammar Snakes & Ladders"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS2-W01","script":"theme. theme. The main theme of the novel is loyalty.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS2-W02","script":"motif. motif. Water is a recurring motif in the poem.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS2-W03","script":"symbolism. symbolism. The novel's symbolism is subtle but powerful.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS2-W04","script":"metaphor. metaphor. \"Time is a thief\" is a metaphor.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Having Been Translated into Forty Languages…: Advanced Participle Clauses”. Let’s learn perfect participle (Having read the novel, …); perfect passive participle (Having been translated into forty languages, …); past participle clauses (Written in 1945, …); being + past participle (Being written in dialect, the poems…); negative participles (Not having read the book, …); conjunction + participle (While reading, When asked, Once published, If left unread, Although written…, Until finished); matching the implied subject to the main clause together. Remember these patterns: Once published, the poem was quoted everywhere. Not having been told about the change, the readers were confused. (Participle clauses are used in statements to combine ideas.) Perfect participle (Having + past participle) shows that one action happened before another: Having read the novel twice, she wrote a brilliant review. Perfect passive (Having been + past participle): Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions of readers. Past participle (passive meaning): Written in 1945, \"Animal Farm\" is still widely read. Being + past participle gives a reason in the passive: Being written in dialect, the poems are difficult for some readers. Negative: put not first: Not having read the book, I could not answer. Not knowing the ending, we were surprised. Many conjunctions can come before a participle: While reading, she took notes. When asked about her inspiration, the author smiled. Once published, the book became a bestseller. If left unread, a book teaches nothing. Although written long ago, the story feels modern. Here are some examples. Having read the novel twice, she wrote a brilliant review. Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions. Written in 1945, \"Animal Farm\" is still widely read. Be careful! Some learners say: “Having translated into many languages, the novel became famous.” That’s not right. We say: “Having been translated into many languages, the novel became famous.” The novel was translated → passive. Quick check: \"Having been translated…\" Did the novel translate, or was it translated? … Think! … It was translated. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Having been translated into forty languages, the novel reached millions of readers.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3","title":"Protagonist, Irony, Foreshadowing: The Language of Literary Analysis","objectives":["Students will be able to analyze stories and poems using precise literary terms.","Students will be able to use analytical verbs (depict, convey, symbolize, foreshadow) accurately.","I can identify the protagonist, the theme and the setting of a story.","I can explain how a writer uses foreshadowing and symbolism."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تحليل القصص والقصائد بمصطلحات أدبية دقيقة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام أفعال التحليل (depict / convey / symbolize / foreshadow) بدقة.","أستطيع أن أحدد البطل والموضوع والزمان والمكان في قصة.","أستطيع أن أشرح كيف يستخدم الكاتب التمهيد والرمزية."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"irony","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈaɪrəni/","definition":"a contrast between what is expected and what actually happens","arabic":"المفارقة / السخرية","example":"It is ironic that the fire station burned down.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W01"},{"word":"foreshadowing","pos":"noun","ipa":"/fɔrˈʃædoʊɪŋ/","definition":"hints in a story about what will happen later","arabic":"التمهيد / الإنذار المسبق","example":"The storm in chapter one is foreshadowing.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W02"},{"word":"allegory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈæləˌɡɔri/","definition":"a story in which characters and events represent deeper meanings","arabic":"قصة رمزية / أليغوريا","example":"\"Animal Farm\" is a political allegory.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W03"},{"word":"satire","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsæˌtaɪɚ/","definition":"the use of humor to criticize people or society","arabic":"الهجاء / السخرية الأدبية","example":"The play is a satire of modern consumer culture.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W04"},{"word":"genre","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈʒɑnrə/","definition":"a type or category of literature, music or film","arabic":"نوع أدبي / جنس","example":"Science fiction is her favorite genre.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W05"},{"word":"climax","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈklaɪˌmæks/","definition":"the most exciting or important point in a story","arabic":"الذروة","example":"The climax comes when the brothers finally meet.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W06"},{"word":"imagery","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪmədʒri/","definition":"descriptive language that creates pictures in the reader's mind","arabic":"الصور الحسية / التصوير","example":"The poem is full of desert imagery.","image_id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Story elements: the protagonist (main character) and antagonist (the opposing character or force); the narrator (who tells the story – a first-person narrator says \"I\"; a third-person narrator says \"he / she\"); the setting (time and place); the plot, with its climax (the most intense point) and turning point; the theme (the central message); the genre (novel, short story, poetry, drama, science fiction…).\nLiterary devices: a metaphor (Time is a thief); imagery (descriptions that appeal to the senses); symbolism (an object stands for an idea: a cage = lack of freedom); a motif (a repeated image or idea); foreshadowing (hints about later events); irony – situational (the opposite of what we expect happens), verbal (saying the opposite of what you mean), dramatic (the audience knows something the character doesn't); an allegory (a story with a hidden meaning); satire (humor used to criticize).\nAnalytical verbs: the writer depicts / portrays a character or scene; conveys a feeling; explores a theme; evokes a mood; an image symbolizes an idea; an early scene foreshadows the ending; the play satirizes society. Use the present simple to write about literature: In the novel, the protagonist leaves his village. (literary present)\nCollocations/phrases: a central theme • a recurring motif • a first-person narrator • a vivid image | depict a character • convey a mood • explore a theme • evoke a feeling | symbolize freedom • foreshadow the ending • satirize society • reach a climax | situational irony • dramatic irony • a political allegory • a turning point","examples":["The protagonist of the novel is a young teacher in Najd.","The setting is a small village in the 1950s.","The novel explores the theme of loyalty.","The caged bird symbolizes the narrator's lack of freedom.","The storm in the first chapter foreshadows the tragedy.","Water is a recurring motif in the poem.","\"Animal Farm\" is a political allegory.","It is ironic that the fire station burned down."],"common_errors":["✗ The protagonist of the story is the bad guy who tries to stop the hero. → ✓ The antagonist is the character who tries to stop the hero. (antagonist = opposing character.)","✗ In the novel, the hero left his village. (literary analysis) → ✓ In the novel, the hero leaves his village. (Literary present.)","✗ The poem symbolizes a metaphor of freedom. → ✓ The bird in the poem symbolizes freedom. (An image symbolizes an idea.)","✗ The ending was foreshadowed in the climax at the end. → ✓ The ending is foreshadowed in the first chapter. (Foreshadowing comes earlier.)"],"arabic":"عناصر القصة: protagonist (البطل) وantagonist (الخصم) وnarrator (الراوي) وsetting (الزمان والمكان) وclimax (الذروة) وtheme (الموضوع) وgenre (النوع الأدبي).\nالأدوات الأدبية: metaphor (استعارة) وimagery (الصور الحسية) وsymbolism (الرمزية) وmotif (الفكرة المتكررة) وforeshadowing (التمهيد) وirony (المفارقة) وallegory (القصة الرمزية) وsatire (الهجاء).\nأفعال التحليل: depict / portray (يصوّر) وconvey (ينقل) وexplore (يستكشف) وsymbolize (يرمز إلى) وforeshadow (يمهّد لـ)، ونكتب عن الأدب بالمضارع البسيط."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Protagonist, Irony, Foreshadowing: The Language of Literary Analysis”. Let’s learn literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U3-LS3 | Protagonist, Irony, Foreshadowing: The Language of Literary Analysis","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. irony: a contrast between what is expected and what actually happens. foreshadowing: hints in a story about what will happen later. allegory: a story in which characters and events represent deeper meanings.","on_screen":"irony | foreshadowing | allegory","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Story elements: the protagonist (main character) and antagonist (the opposing character or force); the narrator (who tells the story – a first-person narrator says \"I\"; a third-person narrator says \"he / she\"); the setting (time and place); the plot, with its climax (the most intense point) and turning point; the theme (the central message); the genre (novel, short story, poetry, drama, science fiction…). Literary devices: a metaphor (Time is a thief); imagery (descriptions that appeal to the senses); symbolism (an object stands for an idea: a cage = lack of freedom); a motif (a repeated image or idea); foreshadowing (hints about later events); irony – situational (the opposite of what we expect happens), verbal (saying the opposite of what you mean), dramatic (the audience knows something the character doesn't); an allegory (a story with a hidden meaning); satire (humor used to criticize).","on_screen":"literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The protagonist of the novel is a young teacher in Najd. The setting is a small village in the 1950s. The novel explores the theme of loyalty.","on_screen":"The protagonist of the novel is a young teacher in Najd. / The setting is a small village in the 1950s. / The novel explores the theme of loyalty.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The protagonist of the story is the bad guy who tries to stop the hero.” That’s not right. We say: “The antagonist is the character who tries to stop the hero.” antagonist = opposing character.","on_screen":"✗ The protagonist of the story is the bad guy who tries to stop the hero.   ✓ The antagonist is the character who tries to stop the hero.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Who tells the story: the narrator or the protagonist? … Think! … The narrator (sometimes the narrator is also the protagonist). Great job! Now you can say: I can identify the protagonist, the theme and the setting of a story.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can identify the protagonist, the theme and the setting of a story.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","عناصر القصة: protagonist (البطل) وantagonist (الخصم) وnarrator (الراوي) وsetting (الزمان والمكان) وclimax (الذروة) وtheme (الموضوع) وgenre (النوع الأدبي).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The protagonist of the story is the bad guy who tries to stop the hero. — الصحيح: The antagonist is the character who tries to stop the hero.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أحدد البطل والموضوع والزمان والمكان في قصة."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"The ___ of the novel is a young teacher who moves to a remote village.","options":["antagonist","narrator's genre","protagonist","climax"],"answer":"protagonist","explanation":"Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."},{"q":"The caged bird in the poem ___ the narrator's lack of freedom.","options":["satirizes","narrates","foreshadows","symbolizes"],"answer":"symbolizes","explanation":"Analytical verb (depict, convey, symbolize…).","explanation_ar":"أفعال التحليل."},{"q":"It is ___ that the fire station burned down.","options":["ironic","allegorical","climactic","metaphorical"],"answer":"ironic","explanation":"Literary device (metaphor, irony, symbolism…).","explanation_ar":"الأدوات الأدبية."},{"q":"The dark clouds in chapter one ___ the tragedy at the end of the novel.","options":["portray as","foreshadow","symbolize","satirize"],"answer":"foreshadow","explanation":"Analytical verb (depict, convey, symbolize…).","explanation_ar":"أفعال التحليل."},{"q":"\"Animal Farm\" is a political ___ in which animals represent real historical figures.","options":["climax","genre","narrator","allegory"],"answer":"allegory","explanation":"Literary device (metaphor, irony, symbolism…).","explanation_ar":"الأدوات الأدبية."},{"q":"Throughout the collection, the poet returns again and again to the image of a well in the desert, a ______ that comes to represent both hope and memory.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["recurring motif","minor antagonist","single climax","brief setting"],"answer":"recurring motif","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the term to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"setting","options":["the time and place in which a story happens","language that creates pictures in the reader's mind","when the audience knows something a character doesn't","the most intense point in a story","a category of literature, such as poetry or drama","humor used to criticize people or society"],"answer":"the time and place in which a story happens","explanation":"setting → the time and place in which a story happens. Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."},{"q":"climax","options":["the time and place in which a story happens","language that creates pictures in the reader's mind","when the audience knows something a character doesn't","the most intense point in a story","a category of literature, such as poetry or drama","humor used to criticize people or society"],"answer":"the most intense point in a story","explanation":"climax → the most intense point in a story. Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."},{"q":"imagery","options":["the time and place in which a story happens","language that creates pictures in the reader's mind","when the audience knows something a character doesn't","the most intense point in a story","a category of literature, such as poetry or drama","humor used to criticize people or society"],"answer":"language that creates pictures in the reader's mind","explanation":"imagery → language that creates pictures in the reader's mind. Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."},{"q":"satire","options":["the time and place in which a story happens","language that creates pictures in the reader's mind","when the audience knows something a character doesn't","the most intense point in a story","a category of literature, such as poetry or drama","humor used to criticize people or society"],"answer":"humor used to criticize people or society","explanation":"satire → humor used to criticize people or society. Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."},{"q":"dramatic irony","options":["the time and place in which a story happens","language that creates pictures in the reader's mind","when the audience knows something a character doesn't","the most intense point in a story","a category of literature, such as poetry or drama","humor used to criticize people or society"],"answer":"when the audience knows something a character doesn't","explanation":"dramatic irony → when the audience knows something a character doesn't. Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."},{"q":"genre","options":["the time and place in which a story happens","language that creates pictures in the reader's mind","when the audience knows something a character doesn't","the most intense point in a story","a category of literature, such as poetry or drama","humor used to criticize people or society"],"answer":"a category of literature, such as poetry or drama","explanation":"genre → a category of literature, such as poetry or drama. Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Analyze a story you know.","items":[{"q":"Name the protagonist and antagonist of a story or film you know.","options":[],"answer":"In the film, the protagonist is a young pilot and the antagonist is a jealous rival.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"What is the setting of your favorite novel?","options":[],"answer":"The setting is a fishing village on the Red Sea coast in the 1960s.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"What theme does the story explore?","options":[],"answer":"The story explores the theme of friendship across generations.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Give an example of a metaphor.","options":[],"answer":"\"My grandmother's house is a treasure chest of memories.\"","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe an example of situational irony.","options":[],"answer":"A traffic safety officer got a speeding ticket on his way to give a talk.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A short literary analysis.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences analyzing a story using 3 literary terms.","options":[],"answer":"The novel is narrated by a first-person narrator. Its central theme is the conflict between tradition and change. The broken clock is a symbol of the family's frozen past.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences using \"foreshadow\" and \"climax\".","options":[],"answer":"The argument in the first chapter foreshadows the family's separation. The climax comes when the brothers meet again after twenty years.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence in the literary present about a character's action.","options":[],"answer":"In the final scene, the narrator returns to her village and plants a palm tree.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Dominoes","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Deal the dominoes equally in teams of four.","Player 1 places a domino; the next player adds a domino whose word matches the open definition.","If you cannot play, pass.","The first player with no dominoes left wins."],"data":[{"word":"protagonist","match":"the main character in a story","arabic":"البطل / الشخصية الرئيسة"},{"word":"antagonist","match":"the character who opposes the main character","arabic":"الخصم / الشخصية المضادة"},{"word":"narrator","match":"the person who tells the story","arabic":"الراوي"},{"word":"setting","match":"the time and place in which a story happens","arabic":"الزمان والمكان / الإطار"},{"word":"theme","match":"the main idea or message of a work of literature","arabic":"الموضوع / الفكرة الرئيسة"},{"word":"motif","match":"an image or idea that is repeated in a work","arabic":"فكرة متكررة / موتيف"},{"word":"symbolism","match":"the use of objects or images to represent ideas","arabic":"الرمزية"},{"word":"metaphor","match":"a comparison that says one thing is another","arabic":"استعارة"},{"word":"irony","match":"a contrast between what is expected and what actually happens","arabic":"المفارقة / السخرية"},{"word":"foreshadowing","match":"hints in a story about what will happen later","arabic":"التمهيد / الإنذار المسبق"},{"word":"allegory","match":"a story in which characters and events represent deeper meanings","arabic":"قصة رمزية / أليغوريا"},{"word":"satire","match":"the use of humor to criticize people or society","arabic":"الهجاء / السخرية الأدبية"},{"word":"genre","match":"a type or category of literature, music or film","arabic":"نوع أدبي / جنس"},{"word":"climax","match":"the most exciting or important point in a story","arabic":"الذروة"},{"word":"imagery","match":"descriptive language that creates pictures in the reader's mind","arabic":"الصور الحسية / التصوير"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The story is told by a ___ narrator who uses \"I\" throughout.","options":["first-hand","third-person","second-hand","first-person"],"answer":"first-person","explanation":"Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."},{"q":"Science fiction is my favorite ___.","options":["setting","climax","motif","genre"],"answer":"genre","explanation":"Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."},{"q":"The poem is full of vivid desert ___: golden dunes, burning sand and cold starlit nights.","options":["allegory","satire","imagery","irony"],"answer":"imagery","explanation":"Literary device (metaphor, irony, symbolism…).","explanation_ar":"الأدوات الأدبية."},{"q":"The play ___ politicians who make promises they never keep.","options":["narrates","symbolizes","foreshadows","satirizes"],"answer":"satirizes","explanation":"Analytical verb (depict, convey, symbolize…).","explanation_ar":"أفعال التحليل."},{"q":"The novel ___ life in Jeddah in the 1970s with great detail.","options":["symbolizes","depicts","satirizes","foreshadows"],"answer":"depicts","explanation":"Analytical verb (depict, convey, symbolize…).","explanation_ar":"أفعال التحليل."},{"q":"\"Time is a thief\" is an example of a ___.","options":["genre","narrator","metaphor","climax"],"answer":"metaphor","explanation":"Literary device (metaphor, irony, symbolism…).","explanation_ar":"الأدوات الأدبية."},{"q":"When the audience knows that the letter is fake but the hero does not, this is ___ irony.","options":["dramatic","situational","allegorical","verbal"],"answer":"dramatic","explanation":"Literary device (metaphor, irony, symbolism…).","explanation_ar":"الأدوات الأدبية."},{"q":"The turning point of the story, when everything changes, is its ___.","options":["climax","genre","motif","setting"],"answer":"climax","explanation":"Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."},{"q":"Rather than stating his message directly, the author uses a story about birds trapped in a golden cage to ______ the idea that comfort can become a kind of prison.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["satirize","convey","narrate","foreshadow"],"answer":"convey","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"In the novel's opening chapter, a minor argument between two brothers ______ the family's later separation, although first-time readers rarely notice it.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["depicts as","foreshadows","satirizes","narrates"],"answer":"foreshadows","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A literature class: a board with a \"story mountain\" diagram (exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, resolution); cards labeled \"protagonist\", \"antagonist\", \"theme\", \"motif\"; students holding novels and highlighting passages. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A literature class: a board with a \"story mountain\" diagram (exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, resolution); cards labeled \"protagonist\", \"antagonist\", \"theme\", \"motif\"; students holding novels and highlighting passages"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows irony (a contrast between what is expected and what actually happens); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing irony: a contrast between what is expected and what actually happens"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows foreshadowing (hints in a story about what will happen later); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing foreshadowing: hints in a story about what will happen later"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows allegory (a story in which characters and events represent deeper meanings); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing allegory: a story in which characters and events represent deeper meanings"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows satire (the use of humor to criticize people or society); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing satire: the use of humor to criticize people or society"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows genre (a type or category of literature, music or film); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing genre: a type or category of literature, music or film"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows climax (the most exciting or important point in a story); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing climax: the most exciting or important point in a story"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows imagery (descriptive language that creates pictures in the reader's mind); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing imagery: descriptive language that creates pictures in the reader's mind"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Dominoes”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Dominoes"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W01","script":"irony. irony. It is ironic that the fire station burned down.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W02","script":"foreshadowing. foreshadowing. The storm in chapter one is foreshadowing.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W03","script":"allegory. allegory. \"Animal Farm\" is a political allegory.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W04","script":"satire. satire. The play is a satire of modern consumer culture.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W05","script":"genre. genre. Science fiction is her favorite genre.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W06","script":"climax. climax. The climax comes when the brothers finally meet.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-W07","script":"imagery. imagery. The poem is full of desert imagery.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Protagonist, Irony, Foreshadowing: The Language of Literary Analysis”. Let’s learn literary terms (protagonist, antagonist, narrator, setting, theme, motif, symbolism, metaphor, irony, foreshadowing, allegory, satire, genre, climax, imagery); types of irony (situational, verbal, dramatic); point of view (first person, third person); verbs for analysis (depict, portray, convey, explore, evoke, symbolize, foreshadow, satirize); collocations (a recurring motif, a central theme, a first-person narrator, a vivid image, a turning point) together. Meet some new words. irony: a contrast between what is expected and what actually happens. foreshadowing: hints in a story about what will happen later. allegory: a story in which characters and events represent deeper meanings. Story elements: the protagonist (main character) and antagonist (the opposing character or force); the narrator (who tells the story – a first-person narrator says \"I\"; a third-person narrator says \"he / she\"); the setting (time and place); the plot, with its climax (the most intense point) and turning point; the theme (the central message); the genre (novel, short story, poetry, drama, science fiction…). Literary devices: a metaphor (Time is a thief); imagery (descriptions that appeal to the senses); symbolism (an object stands for an idea: a cage = lack of freedom); a motif (a repeated image or idea); foreshadowing (hints about later events); irony – situational (the opposite of what we expect happens), verbal (saying the opposite of what you mean), dramatic (the audience knows something the character doesn't); an allegory (a story with a hidden meaning); satire (humor used to criticize). Here are some examples. The protagonist of the novel is a young teacher in Najd. The setting is a small village in the 1950s. The novel explores the theme of loyalty. Be careful! Some learners say: “The protagonist of the story is the bad guy who tries to stop the hero.” That’s not right. We say: “The antagonist is the character who tries to stop the hero.” antagonist = opposing character. Quick check: Who tells the story: the narrator or the protagonist? … Think! … The narrator (sometimes the narrator is also the protagonist). Great job! Now you can say: I can identify the protagonist, the theme and the setting of a story.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS4","title":"Dash, Colon or Parentheses?: Punctuation for Style and Effect","objectives":["Students will be able to choose dashes, colons or parentheses to create a particular effect.","Students will be able to punctuate complex lists and summarizing structures correctly.","I can use a dash for a dramatic afterthought and parentheses for a quiet aside.","I can use semicolons in a list such as \"Cairo, Egypt; Beirut, Lebanon; and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia\"."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من اختيار الشَّرطة أو النقطتين أو الأقواس لإحداث أثر معين.","سيتمكن الطلاب من ترقيم القوائم المعقدة وتراكيب التلخيص بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع أن أستخدم الشَّرطة لإضافة لاحقة مؤثرة والأقواس لملاحظة هادئة.","أستطيع أن أستخدم الفاصلة المنقوطة في قائمة مثل Cairo, Egypt; Beirut, Lebanon; and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"protagonist","pos":"noun","ipa":"/proʊˈtæɡənəst/","definition":"the main character in a story","arabic":"البطل / الشخصية الرئيسة","example":"The protagonist of the novel is a young teacher.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"antagonist","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ænˈtæɡənəst/","definition":"the character who opposes the main character","arabic":"الخصم / الشخصية المضادة","example":"The antagonist tries to stop the hero.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"narrator","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈnɛreɪtɚ/","definition":"the person who tells the story","arabic":"الراوي","example":"The narrator is the hero's younger sister.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"setting","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɛtɪŋ/","definition":"the time and place in which a story happens","arabic":"الزمان والمكان / الإطار","example":"The setting is a village in 1950s Najd.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"theme","pos":"noun","ipa":"/θim/","definition":"the main idea or message of a work of literature","arabic":"الموضوع / الفكرة الرئيسة","example":"The main theme of the novel is loyalty.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"motif","pos":"noun","ipa":"/moʊˈtif/","definition":"an image or idea that is repeated in a work","arabic":"فكرة متكررة / موتيف","example":"Water is a recurring motif in the poem.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Dashes create emphasis or drama: The ending surprised everyone – even the author. They mark an interruption: The novel – and this is rare – has no villain. A dash can sum up a list at the start of a sentence: Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood. Parentheses do the opposite: they make information quieter: The novel (first published in 1984) is still popular. Use them for dates, translations and asides: \"qahwa\" (coffee).\nA colon comes after a complete independent clause and points forward to an explanation, a dramatic reveal, a list or a quotation: The protagonist has only one wish: to see the sea. The poet's message is simple: never forget where you come from. Don't use a colon after a verb or a preposition: (not: The themes are: love and loss.)\nSemicolons separate list items that already contain commas: The festival invited writers from Cairo, Egypt; Beirut, Lebanon; and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia. Choosing for effect: dash = loud and dramatic; parentheses = quiet; colon = formal and explanatory; comma = neutral. Don't overuse dashes – one pair per paragraph is usually enough in formal writing.\ndash = emphasis, interruption, afterthought, summary after a list\nparentheses = quiet aside, date, translation\ncolon = after a complete clause → explanation, reveal, list, quotation\nsemicolons between list items that contain commas","examples":["The ending surprised everyone – even the author.","The novel – and this is rare – has no villain.","Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood.","The poet (born in Abha in 1950) writes about the mountains.","My grandmother made \"qahwa\" (Arabic coffee) every morning.","The protagonist has only one wish: to see the sea.","The poet's message is simple: never forget where you come from.","Writers came from Cairo, Egypt; Beirut, Lebanon; and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia."],"common_errors":["✗ The themes of the novel are: love, loss and hope. → ✓ The themes of the novel are love, loss and hope. (No colon after are.)","✗ Writers came from Cairo, Egypt, Beirut, Lebanon, and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia. → ✓ Writers came from Cairo, Egypt; Beirut, Lebanon; and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia. (Semicolons in a complex list.)","✗ The novel – and this is rare, has no villain. → ✓ The novel – and this is rare – has no villain. (Matching dashes.)","✗ Roses, rain, the call to prayer, these were the sounds of my childhood. → ✓ Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood. (Dash before the summarizing word.)"],"arabic":"الشَّرطة للتوكيد والإثارة: The ending surprised everyone – even the author، وللاعتراض: The novel – and this is rare – has no villain، ولتلخيص قائمة في أول الجملة.\nالأقواس تجعل المعلومة هادئة: للتواريخ والترجمات والملاحظات الجانبية: \"qahwa\" (Arabic coffee).\nالنقطتان بعد جملة مستقلة كاملة للشرح أو الكشف أو القائمة أو الاقتباس، ولا تأتيان بعد الفعل أو حرف الجر.\nالفاصلة المنقوطة تفصل عناصر القائمة التي تحتوي على فواصل، والشَّرطة صاخبة والأقواس هادئة والنقطتان رسمية."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Dash, Colon or Parentheses?: Punctuation for Style and Effect”. Let’s learn choosing punctuation for effect: dashes for emphasis, interruption, a dramatic afterthought or a summary after a list (Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were…); colons for explanation, a dramatic reveal or a formal list after a complete clause; parentheses for de-emphasized asides, dates and translations; semicolons in lists whose items contain commas; avoiding overuse; SAT questions on dashes, colons and parentheses together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U3-LS4 | Dash, Colon or Parentheses?: Punctuation for Style and Effect","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Dashes create emphasis or drama: The ending surprised everyone – even the author. They mark an interruption: The novel – and this is rare – has no villain. A dash can sum up a list at the start of a sentence: Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood. Parentheses do the opposite: they make information quieter: The novel (first published in 1984) is still popular. Use them for dates, translations and asides: \"qahwa\" (coffee).","on_screen":"choosing punctuation for effect: dashes for emphasis, interruption, a dramatic afterthought or a summary after a list (Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were…); colons for explanation, a dramatic reveal or a formal list after a complete clause; parentheses for de-emphasized asides, dates and translations; semicolons in lists whose items contain commas; avoiding overuse; SAT questions on dashes, colons and parentheses","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Dashes create emphasis or drama: The ending surprised everyone – even the author. They mark an interruption: The novel – and this is rare – has no villain. A dash can sum up a list at the start of a sentence: Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood. Parentheses do the opposite: they make information quieter: The novel (first published in 1984) is still popular. Use them for dates, translations and asides: \"qahwa\" (coffee). A colon comes after a complete independent clause and points forward to an explanation, a dramatic reveal, a list or a quotation: The protagonist has only one wish: to see the sea. The poet's message is simple: never forget where you come from. Don't use a colon after a verb or a preposition: (not: The themes are: love and loss.)","on_screen":"dash = emphasis, interruption, afterthought, summary after a list • parentheses = quiet aside, date, translation • colon = after a complete clause → explanation, reveal, list, quotation","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The ending surprised everyone – even the author. The novel – and this is rare – has no villain. Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood.","on_screen":"The ending surprised everyone – even the author. / The novel – and this is rare – has no villain. / Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The themes of the novel are: love, loss and hope.” That’s not right. We say: “The themes of the novel are love, loss and hope.” No colon after are.","on_screen":"✗ The themes of the novel are: love, loss and hope.   ✓ The themes of the novel are love, loss and hope.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Which is louder: a dash or parentheses? … Think! … A dash. Great job! Now you can say: I can use a dash for a dramatic afterthought and parentheses for a quiet aside.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can use a dash for a dramatic afterthought and parentheses for a quiet aside.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: choosing punctuation for effect: dashes for emphasis, interruption, a dramatic afterthought or a summary after a list (Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were…); colons for explanation, a dramatic reveal or a formal list after a complete clause; parentheses for de-emphasized asides, dates and translations; semicolons in lists whose items contain commas; avoiding overuse; SAT questions on dashes, colons and parentheses.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الشَّرطة للتوكيد والإثارة: The ending surprised everyone – even the author، وللاعتراض: The novel – and this is rare – has no villain، ولتلخيص قائمة في أول الجملة.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The themes of the novel are: love, loss and hope. — الصحيح: The themes of the novel are love, loss and hope.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أستخدم الشَّرطة لإضافة لاحقة مؤثرة والأقواس لملاحظة هادئة."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Review of a Classic Novel (editing passage)","body":"The novel, (first published in 1984) tells the story of a desert town. The setting is described in rich detail the heat, the dust and the silence. The protagonist – a young boy named Mut'ib, changes completely by the end. The book explores three themes; tradition, change and loss. Reading it, I felt one emotion above all, sadness.","word_count":58,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the best punctuation.","items":[{"q":"The protagonist has only one ______ to see the sea before she dies.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["wish:","wish – and","wish;","wish, and"],"answer":"wish:","explanation":"Colon after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"","options":["The novel – and this is rare, has no villain.","The novel – and this is rare has no villain.","The novel – and this is rare – has no villain.","The novel, and this is rare – has no villain."],"answer":"The novel – and this is rare – has no villain.","explanation":"Dash for emphasis, interruption or afterthought.","explanation_ar":"الشَّرطة."},{"q":"","options":["Roses, rain, the call to prayer, these were the sounds of my childhood.","Roses, rain, the call to prayer: these, were the sounds of my childhood.","Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood.","Roses, rain, the call to prayer; these were the sounds of my childhood."],"answer":"Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood.","explanation":"Dash after a list that is summarized.","explanation_ar":"الشَّرطة بعد القائمة."},{"q":"","options":["My grandmother made \"qahwa\" (Arabic coffee every morning.","My grandmother made \"qahwa\" Arabic coffee) every morning.","My grandmother made (\"qahwa\" Arabic coffee) every morning.","My grandmother made \"qahwa\" (Arabic coffee) every morning."],"answer":"My grandmother made \"qahwa\" (Arabic coffee) every morning.","explanation":"Parentheses for quiet asides, dates, translations.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."},{"q":"","options":["The book fair invited writers from Cairo; Egypt, Beirut; Lebanon, and Riyadh; Saudi Arabia.","The book fair invited writers from Cairo, Egypt; Beirut, Lebanon; and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia.","The book fair invited writers from Cairo, Egypt, Beirut, Lebanon, and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia.","The book fair invited writers from: Cairo, Egypt; Beirut, Lebanon; and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia."],"answer":"The book fair invited writers from Cairo, Egypt; Beirut, Lebanon; and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia.","explanation":"Semicolons in complex lists.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة المنقوطة في القوائم."},{"q":"The final chapter contains a twist that no reader ______ the narrator has been dead since the first page.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["expects:","expects,","expects; and","expects – and"],"answer":"expects:","explanation":"Colon after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS4-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the punctuation.","items":[{"q":"The main themes of the novel are: love, loss and hope.","options":[],"answer":"The main themes of the novel are love, loss and hope.","explanation":"are: love → are love. Colon after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: are: love → are love. النقطتان."},{"q":"The novel – first published in 1984, is still widely read.","options":[],"answer":"The novel(first published in 1984) is still widely read.","explanation":"– first published in 1984, is → (first published in 1984) is. Parentheses for quiet asides, dates, translations.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح:  – first published in 1984, is → (first published in 1984) is. الأقواس."},{"q":"The ending shocked everyone, even the author.","options":[],"answer":"The ending shocked everyone– even the author.","explanation":", → –. Dash for emphasis, interruption or afterthought.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → –. الشَّرطة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the punctuation.","items":[{"q":"The festival honored poets from Jeddah, Saudi Arabia, Muscat, Oman; and Kuwait City, Kuwait.","options":[],"answer":"The festival honored poets from Jeddah, Saudi Arabia; Muscat, Oman; and Kuwait City, Kuwait.","explanation":"Arabia, Muscat → Arabia; Muscat. Semicolons in complex lists.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Arabia, Muscat → Arabia; Muscat. الفاصلة المنقوطة في القوائم."},{"q":"Courage, loyalty, sacrifice, these are the values the hero represents.","options":[],"answer":"Courage, loyalty, sacrifice– these are the values the hero represents.","explanation":", → –. Dash after a list that is summarized.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → –. الشَّرطة بعد القائمة."},{"q":"The poet's message is simple never forget where you come from.","options":[],"answer":"The poet's message is simple: never forget where you come from.","explanation":"simple never → simple: never. Colon after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: simple never → simple: never. النقطتان."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the book review.","items":[{"q":"The novel, (first published in 1984) tells the story of a desert town.","options":[],"answer":"The novel (first published in 1984) tells the story of a desert town.","explanation":", → (delete). Dash for emphasis, interruption or afterthought.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). الشَّرطة."},{"q":"The setting is described in rich detail the heat, the dust and the silence.","options":[],"answer":"The setting is described in rich detail: the heat, the dust and the silence.","explanation":"detail the → detail: the. Dash for emphasis, interruption or afterthought.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: detail the → detail: the. الشَّرطة."},{"q":"The protagonist – a young boy named Mut'ib, changes completely by the end.","options":[],"answer":"The protagonist– a young boy named Mut'ib – changes completely by the end.","explanation":"– a young boy named Mut'ib, changes → – a young boy named Mut'ib – changes. Dash for emphasis, interruption or afterthought.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح:  – a young boy named Mut'ib, changes → – a young boy named Mut'ib – changes. الشَّرطة."},{"q":"The book explores three themes; tradition, change and loss.","options":[],"answer":"The book explores three themes: tradition, change and loss.","explanation":"themes; tradition → themes: tradition. Dash for emphasis, interruption or afterthought.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: themes; tradition → themes: tradition. الشَّرطة."},{"q":"Reading it, I felt one emotion above all, sadness.","options":[],"answer":"Reading it, I felt one emotion above all: sadness.","explanation":", → :. Dash for emphasis, interruption or afterthought.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → :. الشَّرطة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write sentences for effect.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with a dramatic dash at the end.","options":[],"answer":"The hero finally opened the door – and found it empty.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing punctuation for effect: dashes for emphasis, interruption, a dramatic afterthought or a summary after a list (Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were…); colons for explanation, a dramatic reveal or a formal list after a complete clause; parentheses for de-emphasized asides, dates and translations; semicolons in lists whose items contain commas; avoiding overuse; SAT questions on dashes, colons and parentheses correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with parentheses for a translation or date.","options":[],"answer":"We ate \"kabsa\" (spiced rice with meat) at my aunt's house.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing punctuation for effect: dashes for emphasis, interruption, a dramatic afterthought or a summary after a list (Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were…); colons for explanation, a dramatic reveal or a formal list after a complete clause; parentheses for de-emphasized asides, dates and translations; semicolons in lists whose items contain commas; avoiding overuse; SAT questions on dashes, colons and parentheses correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence that summarizes a list with a dash.","options":[],"answer":"Books, notebooks, a pen that never worked – these were my grandfather's treasures.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing punctuation for effect: dashes for emphasis, interruption, a dramatic afterthought or a summary after a list (Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were…); colons for explanation, a dramatic reveal or a formal list after a complete clause; parentheses for de-emphasized asides, dates and translations; semicolons in lists whose items contain commas; avoiding overuse; SAT questions on dashes, colons and parentheses correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Speed Spell","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["The teacher says a word and a short sentence with it.","Students write the word in 10 seconds.","Show boards on \"Show me!\". Correct = 1 point.","After 10 words, partners check each other's boards and correct any mistakes."],"data":[{"word":"protagonist","clue":"the main character in a story"},{"word":"antagonist","clue":"the character who opposes the main character"},{"word":"foreshadowing","clue":"hints in a story about what will happen later"},{"word":"allegory","clue":"a story in which characters and events represent deeper meanings"},{"word":"symbolism","clue":"the use of objects or images to represent ideas"},{"word":"imagery","clue":"descriptive language that creates pictures in the reader's mind"},{"word":"narrator","clue":"the person who tells the story"},{"word":"setting","clue":"the time and place in which a story happens"},{"word":"theme","clue":"the main idea or message of a work of literature"},{"word":"motif","clue":"an image or idea that is repeated in a work"},{"word":"metaphor","clue":"a comparison that says one thing is another"},{"word":"irony","clue":"a contrast between what is expected and what actually happens"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["The poem has one simple message, be kind, and.","The poem has one simple message: be kind.","The poem has one simple message; be kind.","The poem has: one simple message be kind."],"answer":"The poem has one simple message: be kind.","explanation":"Colon after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"","options":["The play was a success – until the final scene.","The play was a success until – the final scene.","The play was a success, – until the final scene.","The play was a success – until, the final scene."],"answer":"The play was a success – until the final scene.","explanation":"Dash for emphasis, interruption or afterthought.","explanation_ar":"الشَّرطة."},{"q":"","options":["Patience, faith, hope – these are the themes of the story.","Patience, faith, hope: these, are the themes of the story.","Patience, faith, hope, these are the themes of the story.","Patience faith hope – these are the themes of the story."],"answer":"Patience, faith, hope – these are the themes of the story.","explanation":"Dash after a list that is summarized.","explanation_ar":"الشَّرطة بعد القائمة."},{"q":"","options":["The (author who rarely gives interviews) spoke at the festival.","The author (who rarely gives interviews) spoke at the festival.","The author who rarely gives interviews) spoke at the festival.","The author (who rarely gives interviews spoke at the festival."],"answer":"The author (who rarely gives interviews) spoke at the festival.","explanation":"Parentheses for quiet asides, dates, translations.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."},{"q":"","options":["The tour visited Fez, Morocco, Tunis, Tunisia, and Amman, Jordan.","The tour visited: Fez, Morocco; Tunis, Tunisia; and Amman, Jordan.","The tour visited Fez; Morocco, Tunis; Tunisia, and Amman; Jordan.","The tour visited Fez, Morocco; Tunis, Tunisia; and Amman, Jordan."],"answer":"The tour visited Fez, Morocco; Tunis, Tunisia; and Amman, Jordan.","explanation":"Semicolons in complex lists.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة المنقوطة في القوائم."},{"q":"Which punctuation makes an aside sound quieter and less important?","options":["a colon","a dash","parentheses","an exclamation mark"],"answer":"parentheses","explanation":"Choosing punctuation for effect.","explanation_ar":"الترقيم والأثر."},{"q":"Which punctuation is the most dramatic for an afterthought?","options":["a dash","a comma","parentheses","a semicolon"],"answer":"a dash","explanation":"Choosing punctuation for effect.","explanation_ar":"الترقيم والأثر."},{"q":"The young poet's first collection received an unusual honor ______ it was added to the national school curriculum only a year after its publication.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["; and",",",":","and:"],"answer":":","explanation":"Colon after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"The ancient poem ______ was rediscovered in a private library in Istanbul in the nineteenth century.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["(lost for more than six hundred years,",", lost for more than six hundred years)","lost for more than six hundred years)","(lost for more than six hundred years)"],"answer":"(lost for more than six hundred years)","explanation":"Parentheses for quiet asides, dates, translations.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."},{"q":"Mahfouz's novels were translated into many ______ English, French, German and Spanish.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["languages including:","languages; including","languages: including","languages, including"],"answer":"languages, including","explanation":"Colon after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Literature and Society” that shows choosing punctuation for effect: dashes for emphasis, interruption, a dramatic afterthought or a summary after a list (Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were…); colons for explanation, a dramatic reveal or a formal list after a complete clause; parentheses for de-emphasized asides, dates and translations; semicolons in lists whose items contain commas; avoiding overuse; SAT questions on dashes, colons and parentheses in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Literature and Society” that shows choosing punctuation for effect: dashes for emphasis, interruption, a dramatic afterthought or a summary after a list (Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were…); colons for explanation, a dramatic reveal or a formal list after a complete clause; parentheses for de-emphasized asides, dates and translations; semicolons in lists whose items contain commas; avoiding overuse; SAT questions on dashes, colons and parentheses in action"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Speed Spell”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Speed Spell"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Dash, Colon or Parentheses?: Punctuation for Style and Effect”. Let’s learn choosing punctuation for effect: dashes for emphasis, interruption, a dramatic afterthought or a summary after a list (Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were…); colons for explanation, a dramatic reveal or a formal list after a complete clause; parentheses for de-emphasized asides, dates and translations; semicolons in lists whose items contain commas; avoiding overuse; SAT questions on dashes, colons and parentheses together. Dashes create emphasis or drama: The ending surprised everyone – even the author. They mark an interruption: The novel – and this is rare – has no villain. A dash can sum up a list at the start of a sentence: Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood. Parentheses do the opposite: they make information quieter: The novel (first published in 1984) is still popular. Use them for dates, translations and asides: \"qahwa\" (coffee). Dashes create emphasis or drama: The ending surprised everyone – even the author. They mark an interruption: The novel – and this is rare – has no villain. A dash can sum up a list at the start of a sentence: Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood. Parentheses do the opposite: they make information quieter: The novel (first published in 1984) is still popular. Use them for dates, translations and asides: \"qahwa\" (coffee). A colon comes after a complete independent clause and points forward to an explanation, a dramatic reveal, a list or a quotation: The protagonist has only one wish: to see the sea. The poet's message is simple: never forget where you come from. Don't use a colon after a verb or a preposition: (not: The themes are: love and loss.) Here are some examples. The ending surprised everyone – even the author. The novel – and this is rare – has no villain. Roses, rain, the call to prayer – these were the sounds of my childhood. Be careful! Some learners say: “The themes of the novel are: love, loss and hope.” That’s not right. We say: “The themes of the novel are love, loss and hope.” No colon after are. Quick check: Which is louder: a dash or parentheses? … Think! … A dash. Great job! Now you can say: I can use a dash for a dramatic afterthought and parentheses for a quiet aside.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS5","title":"A Book That Changed How I See the World: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to present a book or poem that has influenced them.","Students will be able to write a literary response essay with absolute and participle constructions, precise literary terms and effective punctuation, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can talk about a book that influenced me.","I can write a literary response essay."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تقديم كتاب أو قصيدة أثّرت فيهم.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة مقال استجابة أدبية بالتراكيب المطلقة وجمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول والمصطلحات الأدبية الدقيقة والترقيم المؤثر، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أتحدث عن كتاب أثّر فيّ.","أستطيع أن أكتب مقال استجابة أدبية."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"protagonist","pos":"noun","ipa":"/proʊˈtæɡənəst/","definition":"the main character in a story","arabic":"البطل / الشخصية الرئيسة","example":"The protagonist of the novel is a young teacher.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"antagonist","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ænˈtæɡənəst/","definition":"the character who opposes the main character","arabic":"الخصم / الشخصية المضادة","example":"The antagonist tries to stop the hero.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"narrator","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈnɛreɪtɚ/","definition":"the person who tells the story","arabic":"الراوي","example":"The narrator is the hero's younger sister.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"setting","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɛtɪŋ/","definition":"the time and place in which a story happens","arabic":"الزمان والمكان / الإطار","example":"The setting is a village in 1950s Najd.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"theme","pos":"noun","ipa":"/θim/","definition":"the main idea or message of a work of literature","arabic":"الموضوع / الفكرة الرئيسة","example":"The main theme of the novel is loyalty.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"motif","pos":"noun","ipa":"/moʊˈtif/","definition":"an image or idea that is repeated in a work","arabic":"فكرة متكررة / موتيف","example":"Water is a recurring motif in the poem.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"symbolism","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɪmbəˌlɪzəm/","definition":"the use of objects or images to represent ideas","arabic":"الرمزية","example":"The novel's symbolism is subtle but powerful.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"metaphor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmɛtəfɔr/","definition":"a comparison that says one thing is another","arabic":"استعارة","example":"\"Time is a thief\" is a metaphor.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"irony","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈaɪrəni/","definition":"a contrast between what is expected and what actually happens","arabic":"المفارقة / السخرية","example":"It is ironic that the fire station burned down.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"foreshadowing","pos":"noun","ipa":"/fɔrˈʃædoʊɪŋ/","definition":"hints in a story about what will happen later","arabic":"التمهيد / الإنذار المسبق","example":"The storm in chapter one is foreshadowing.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"allegory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈæləˌɡɔri/","definition":"a story in which characters and events represent deeper meanings","arabic":"قصة رمزية / أليغوريا","example":"\"Animal Farm\" is a political allegory.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"satire","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsæˌtaɪɚ/","definition":"the use of humor to criticize people or society","arabic":"الهجاء / السخرية الأدبية","example":"The play is a satire of modern consumer culture.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"genre","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈʒɑnrə/","definition":"a type or category of literature, music or film","arabic":"نوع أدبي / جنس","example":"Science fiction is her favorite genre.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"climax","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈklaɪˌmæks/","definition":"the most exciting or important point in a story","arabic":"الذروة","example":"The climax comes when the brothers finally meet.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"imagery","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪmədʒri/","definition":"descriptive language that creates pictures in the reader's mind","arabic":"الصور الحسية / التصوير","example":"The poem is full of desert imagery.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 3: absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, and punctuation for style.\nA literary response essay introduces the work (title, author, date, genre), summarizes the plot briefly in the literary present, analyzes literary devices (symbolism, irony, imagery), and explains the personal impact of the work. Absolute and participle constructions make descriptions vivid and concise.\n✔ His hands bleeding, he… • The work completed, …\n✔ Written in 1951, … • Having caught it, … • When asked, …\n✔ protagonist, theme, irony, symbolize, foreshadow\n✔ dash for emphasis • colon for a reveal • literary present","examples":["Written in 1951, the novel is short but powerful.","Its protagonist is an old fisherman.","His hands bleeding, he fights the giant fish.","Having finally caught it, he ties it to his boat.","The ending is full of irony: he returns with nothing.","He is not defeated – only tired.","The marlin symbolizes the goals we struggle for.","In the end, Santiago returns to his village. (literary present)"],"common_errors":["✗ Writing in 1951, the novel is short. → ✓ Written in 1951, the novel is short. (The novel was written.)","✗ His hands bled, he fights the marlin. → ✓ His hands bleeding, he fights the marlin. (Absolute construction.)","✗ In the end, Santiago returned with nothing. (analysis) → ✓ In the end, Santiago returns with nothing. (Literary present.)"],"arabic":"نراجع التراكيب المطلقة وجمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول المتقدمة والمصطلحات الأدبية والترقيم الأسلوبي.\nمقال الاستجابة الأدبية يعرّف العمل (العنوان والمؤلف والتاريخ والنوع)، ويلخص الحبكة بالمضارع، ويحلل الأدوات الأدبية، ويشرح الأثر الشخصي.\nالتراكيب المطلقة وجمل اسم الفاعل تجعل الوصف حيًّا وموجزًا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Book That Changed How I See the World: Language in Use”. Let’s learn absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U3-LS5 | A Book That Changed How I See the World: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Book talk: choose a book, story or poem that changed how you see something. Give a 2-minute talk including one absolute construction, one participle clause (Having read…, Written in…), three literary terms and one sentence with a dash or colon for effect. Classmates ask one question each.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 3: absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, and punctuation for style. A literary response essay introduces the work (title, author, date, genre), summarizes the plot briefly in the literary present, analyzes literary devices (symbolism, irony, imagery), and explains the personal impact of the work. Absolute and participle constructions make descriptions vivid and concise.","on_screen":"✔ His hands bleeding, he… • The work completed, … • ✔ Written in 1951, … • Having caught it, … • When asked, … • ✔ protagonist, theme, irony, symbolize, foreshadow","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Written in 1951, the novel is short but powerful. Its protagonist is an old fisherman. His hands bleeding, he fights the giant fish.","on_screen":"Written in 1951, the novel is short but powerful. / Its protagonist is an old fisherman. / His hands bleeding, he fights the giant fish.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Writing in 1951, the novel is short.” That’s not right. We say: “Written in 1951, the novel is short.” The novel was written.","on_screen":"✗ Writing in 1951, the novel is short.   ✓ Written in 1951, the novel is short.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “His hands bled, he fights the marlin.”? … Think! … It should be “His hands bleeding, he fights the marlin.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can talk about a book that influenced me.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can talk about a book that influenced me.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع التراكيب المطلقة وجمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول المتقدمة والمصطلحات الأدبية والترقيم الأسلوبي.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Writing in 1951, the novel is short. — الصحيح: Written in 1951, the novel is short.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أتحدث عن كتاب أثّر فيّ."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"The Old Man and the Sea – Sara","body":"Written in 1951, Ernest Hemingway's \"The Old Man and the Sea\" is a short novel with a powerful theme.\nIts protagonist, Santiago, is an old Cuban fisherman who has not caught a fish for eighty-four days.\nHis hands bleeding and his body exhausted, he fights a giant marlin for three days.\nHaving finally caught it, he ties it to his boat, but sharks destroy it on the way home.\nThe ending is full of irony: Santiago returns with nothing but a skeleton.\nYet the novel suggests that he is not defeated – only tired.\nThe marlin symbolizes the great goals we struggle for, and the sharks symbolize the forces that take them away.\nHaving read the book, I now believe that how we fight matters as much as whether we win.","word_count":131,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Sara's response essay. True or False?","items":[{"q":"\"The Old Man and the Sea\" was written in 1951.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “The Old Man and the Sea – Sara”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The protagonist is a young fisherman.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “The Old Man and the Sea – Sara”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Santiago fights the marlin for three days.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “The Old Man and the Sea – Sara”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"He brings the whole fish home safely.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “The Old Man and the Sea – Sara”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Sara says the ending contains irony.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “The Old Man and the Sea – Sara”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Sara believes winning is the only thing that matters.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “The Old Man and the Sea – Sara”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"___ in 1951, the novel is short but powerful.","options":["Writing","Written","Having written","Being writing"],"answer":"Written","explanation":"Absolute or participle construction.","explanation_ar":"التراكيب."},{"q":"His hands ___, he fights the giant marlin for three days.","options":["bleeding","were bleeding","bled","bleed"],"answer":"bleeding","explanation":"Absolute or participle construction.","explanation_ar":"التراكيب."},{"q":"___ caught the fish, he ties it to his boat.","options":["Finally having been","Having been finally","Having finally","Being finally"],"answer":"Having finally","explanation":"Absolute or participle construction.","explanation_ar":"التراكيب."},{"q":"The marlin ___ the great goals we struggle for.","options":["narrates","symbolizes","foreshadows","satirizes"],"answer":"symbolizes","explanation":"Literary term or analytical verb.","explanation_ar":"المصطلحات الأدبية."},{"q":"","options":["The ending is full of: irony Santiago returns with nothing but a skeleton.","The ending is full of irony Santiago returns with nothing but a skeleton.","The ending is full of irony; and Santiago returns with nothing but a skeleton.","The ending is full of irony: Santiago returns with nothing but a skeleton."],"answer":"The ending is full of irony: Santiago returns with nothing but a skeleton.","explanation":"Punctuation for effect.","explanation_ar":"الترقيم."},{"q":"Santiago loses the marlin to the sharks. ______ the novel presents him not as a failure but as a man who refused to give up.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Similarly,","For example,","Therefore,","Nevertheless,"],"answer":"Nevertheless,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Prepare your book talk.","items":[{"q":"Title, author, date and genre with a participle clause: ___","options":[],"answer":"Published in 1988, \"The Alchemist\" by Paulo Coelho is a short novel.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"An absolute construction describing the protagonist: ___","options":[],"answer":"His sheep left behind, the young shepherd sets off across the desert.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence with a literary term: ___","options":[],"answer":"The treasure symbolizes each person's dream.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence with a dash or colon for effect: ___","options":[],"answer":"The ending is surprising: the treasure was at home all along.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"The personal impact: ___","options":[],"answer":"Having read it, I began to take my own dreams more seriously.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your literary response essay.","items":[{"q":"Plan: introduction (title, author, date, genre), plot, devices, personal impact.","options":[],"answer":"\"Cities of Salt\", Abdul Rahman Munif, 1984, novel; oil discovered in a desert oasis; symbolism of the bulldozers, irony; I understood the cost of rapid change","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your response essay on the book, story or poem you chose (8–9 sentences).","options":[],"answer":"Published in 1984, Abdul Rahman Munif's \"Cities of Salt\" is a novel about the arrival of the oil industry in a desert community. The setting is a peaceful oasis called Wadi al-Uyoun. Its trees cut down and its springs destroyed, the oasis changes beyond recognition. The protagonist, Mut'ib al-Hathal, warns his neighbors about the newcomers, but few listen to him. Having disappeared into the desert, he becomes almost a legend. The bulldozers symbolize a change that cannot be stopped – and that nobody fully understands. The novel's central theme is clear: progress always has a price. Having read it, I see my own country's rapid transformation with new eyes.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: absolute constructions with their own subjects? participles not dangling? literary present? dash and colon used correctly? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"Publishing in 1984\" to \"Published in 1984\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spin the Review Wheel","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["A player spins the wheel and lands on a word.","Say what the word means and use it in a sentence with absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","Teammates check: meaning (1 point), grammar (1 point), pronunciation (1 point).","Highest total after 6 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"protagonist","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"antagonist","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"narrator","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"setting","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"theme","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"motif","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"symbolism","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"metaphor","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"irony","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"foreshadowing","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"allegory","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"satire","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"genre","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"climax","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"imagery","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___ by readers around the world, the poem has been translated into thirty languages.","options":["Being loving","Loved","Loving","Having loved"],"answer":"Loved","explanation":"Absolute or participle construction.","explanation_ar":"التراكيب."},{"q":"The battle over, the soldiers ___ home.","options":["returning","returned","were return","to return"],"answer":"returned","explanation":"Absolute or participle construction.","explanation_ar":"التراكيب."},{"q":"The novel's central ___ is the conflict between tradition and change.","options":["genre","narrator","theme","climax"],"answer":"theme","explanation":"Literary term or analytical verb.","explanation_ar":"المصطلحات الأدبية."},{"q":"In the final chapter, the protagonist ___ to her village. (literary present)","options":["was returning","returned","had returned","returns"],"answer":"returns","explanation":"Literary term or analytical verb.","explanation_ar":"المصطلحات الأدبية."},{"q":"","options":["The hero had one goal: to save his village.","The hero had one goal, to save: his village.","The hero had: one goal to save his village.","The hero had one goal; to save his village."],"answer":"The hero had one goal: to save his village.","explanation":"Punctuation for effect.","explanation_ar":"الترقيم."},{"q":"The first half of the novel moves slowly. ______ the second half is full of action and surprising twists.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["By contrast,","Therefore,","As a result,","Similarly,"],"answer":"By contrast,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"The author grew up in a fishing village on the Red Sea. ______ the sea appears in almost every one of her stories.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["In contrast,","Nevertheless,","Perhaps for this reason,","However,"],"answer":"Perhaps for this reason,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Naguib Mahfouz was an Egyptian novelist.\n• He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1988.\n• He was the first Arabic-language writer to win the prize.\n• His novels describe everyday life in Cairo.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize the historic importance of Mahfouz's Nobel Prize. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Mahfouz was a novelist.","The Nobel Prize is awarded every year.","Mahfouz's novels describe life in Cairo.","In 1988, the Egyptian novelist Naguib Mahfouz became the first Arabic-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature."],"answer":"In 1988, the Egyptian novelist Naguib Mahfouz became the first Arabic-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• \"Animal Farm\" was written by George Orwell.\n• It was published in 1945.\n• In the story, farm animals overthrow their human owner.\n• The story is an allegory about the dangers of absolute power.\n\nThe student wants to explain what kind of story \"Animal Farm\" is. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["George Orwell was a writer.","Many stories include animals.","\"Animal Farm\" was published in 1945.","\"Animal Farm,\" published by George Orwell in 1945, is an allegory in which farm animals overthrow their owner, warning readers about the dangers of absolute power."],"answer":"\"Animal Farm,\" published by George Orwell in 1945, is an allegory in which farm animals overthrow their owner, warning readers about the dangers of absolute power.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best opening sentence for a literary response essay:","options":["\"The Old Man and the Sea\", it's a short novel – with: a powerful theme.","Written in 1951, \"The Old Man and the Sea\" is a short novel with a powerful theme.","The old man and the sea, written 1951, a short novel.","Writing in 1951, \"The Old Man and the Sea\" is a short novel with a powerful theme."],"answer":"Written in 1951, \"The Old Man and the Sea\" is a short novel with a powerful theme.","explanation":"Absolute or participle construction.","explanation_ar":"التراكيب."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A school \"Book Talk\" event in the library: Sara holding a worn copy of a novel, speaking to classmates seated in a circle; a display \"Books That Changed Us\"; posters of famous Arab and world authors. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A school \"Book Talk\" event in the library: Sara holding a worn copy of a novel, speaking to classmates seated in a circle; a display \"Books That Changed Us\"; posters of famous Arab and world authors"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Literature and Society”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Literature and Society”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L12-U3-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spin the Review Wheel”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spin the Review Wheel"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS5-T1","script":"Written in 1951, Ernest Hemingway's \"The Old Man and the Sea\" is a short novel with a powerful theme. Its protagonist, Santiago, is an old Cuban fisherman who has not caught a fish for eighty-four days. His hands bleeding and his body exhausted, he fights a giant marlin for three days. Having finally caught it, he ties it to his boat, but sharks destroy it on the way home. The ending is full of irony: Santiago returns with nothing but a skeleton. Yet the novel suggests that he is not defeated – only tired. The marlin symbolizes the great goals we struggle for, and the sharks symbolize the forces that take them away. Having read the book, I now believe that how we fight matters as much as whether we win.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U3-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Book That Changed How I See the World: Language in Use”. Let’s learn absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, stylistic punctuation; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Book talk: choose a book, story or poem that changed how you see something. Give a 2-minute talk including one absolute construction, one participle clause (Having read…, Written in…), three literary terms and one sentence with a dash or colon for effect. Classmates ask one question each. Today you use all of Unit 3: absolute constructions, advanced participle clauses, literary terms and analytical verbs, and punctuation for style. A literary response essay introduces the work (title, author, date, genre), summarizes the plot briefly in the literary present, analyzes literary devices (symbolism, irony, imagery), and explains the personal impact of the work. Absolute and participle constructions make descriptions vivid and concise. Here are some examples. Written in 1951, the novel is short but powerful. Its protagonist is an old fisherman. His hands bleeding, he fights the giant fish. Be careful! Some learners say: “Writing in 1951, the novel is short.” That’s not right. We say: “Written in 1951, the novel is short.” The novel was written. Quick check: what is wrong with “His hands bled, he fights the marlin.”? … Think! … It should be “His hands bleeding, he fights the marlin.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can talk about a book that influenced me.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"Her voice ___, the poet read the final lines.","options":["trembled","trembling","trembles","was trembling"],"answer":"trembling","explanation":"Noun + -ing participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ing."},{"q":"The manuscript ___, the author sent it to her publisher.","options":["completed","completes","was completed","completing it"],"answer":"completed","explanation":"Noun + -ed participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ed."},{"q":"___ in hand, the old storyteller began.","options":["Book","A book is","The book was","Books"],"answer":"Book","explanation":"Noun + adjective / phrase / adverb.","explanation_ar":"اسم + صفة أو شبه جملة."},{"q":"With the audience ___, the actor stepped onto the stage.","options":["watched","watching","was watching","watches"],"answer":"watching","explanation":"with / without + noun + modifier.","explanation_ar":"with + اسم."},{"q":"That ___, the novel's ending is disappointing.","options":["being said","said being","been said","is said"],"answer":"being said","explanation":"Fixed absolute expressions.","explanation_ar":"العبارات الثابتة."},{"q":"The library's oldest manuscript was carefully placed in a glass case, ______ under soft lights that would not damage the ancient ink.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["it displayed its pages","its pages display","its pages were displayed","its pages displayed"],"answer":"its pages displayed","explanation":"Noun + -ed participle.","explanation_ar":"اسم + -ed."},{"q":"___ all of her novels, I can say that this is her best.","options":["Having read","Having been read","Reading of","Being read"],"answer":"Having read","explanation":"Having + past participle (earlier action).","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"___ into many languages, her poems are known around the world.","options":["Been translating","Translating","Having been translated","Having translated"],"answer":"Having been translated","explanation":"Having been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"Having been done."},{"q":"___ in a small coastal town, the story is narrated by a young fisherman.","options":["Setting","Having set","Set","Being setting"],"answer":"Set","explanation":"Past participle clause (Written in…).","explanation_ar":"Written in…"},{"q":"___ the first book in the series, I was confused by the second.","options":["Not read","Having not read","Having read not","Not having read"],"answer":"Not having read","explanation":"Not having… / Not knowing…","explanation_ar":"النفي."},{"q":"When ______ why she began writing, the novelist explained that her grandmother's stories had inspired her to record the history of her village.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["asking","asked","having asked her","she asked"],"answer":"asked","explanation":"Conjunction + participle (When asked, Once published).","explanation_ar":"الأداة + اسم الفاعل."},{"q":"The ___ of the story is the character who opposes the hero.","options":["protagonist","narrator","antagonist","genre"],"answer":"antagonist","explanation":"Story element (protagonist, setting, climax…).","explanation_ar":"عناصر القصة."},{"q":"The broken clock in the novel ___ the family's frozen past.","options":["foreshadows","symbolizes","satirizes","narrates"],"answer":"symbolizes","explanation":"Analytical verb (depict, convey, symbolize…).","explanation_ar":"أفعال التحليل."},{"q":"\"The fire station burned down\" is an example of ___ irony.","options":["allegorical","situational","verbal","dramatic"],"answer":"situational","explanation":"Literary device (metaphor, irony, symbolism…).","explanation_ar":"الأدوات الأدبية."},{"q":"Early in the play, a character casually mentions that the bridge is unsafe, a detail that ______ the accident in the final act.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["symbolizes as","satirizes","narrates","foreshadows"],"answer":"foreshadows","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The poem is rich in desert ___: golden dunes, burning sand and silent stars.","options":["imagery","irony","satire","allegory"],"answer":"imagery","explanation":"Literary device (metaphor, irony, symbolism…).","explanation_ar":"الأدوات الأدبية."},{"q":"","options":["The novel – and this is unusual, has no named characters.","The novel, and this is unusual – has no named characters.","The novel – and this is unusual has no named characters.","The novel – and this is unusual – has no named characters."],"answer":"The novel – and this is unusual – has no named characters.","explanation":"Dash for emphasis, interruption or afterthought.","explanation_ar":"الشَّرطة."},{"q":"","options":["The festival welcomed poets from Rabat, Morocco, Doha, Qatar, and Abha, Saudi Arabia.","The festival welcomed poets from: Rabat, Morocco; Doha, Qatar; and Abha, Saudi Arabia.","The festival welcomed poets from Rabat, Morocco; Doha, Qatar; and Abha, Saudi Arabia.","The festival welcomed poets from Rabat; Morocco, Doha; Qatar, and Abha; Saudi Arabia."],"answer":"The festival welcomed poets from Rabat, Morocco; Doha, Qatar; and Abha, Saudi Arabia.","explanation":"Semicolons in complex lists.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة المنقوطة في القوائم."},{"q":"The novel's final sentence contains a surprising ______ the narrator has been writing to herself all along.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["revelation:","revelation,","revelation – and","revelation; and"],"answer":"revelation:","explanation":"Colon after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"Many critics praised the novel's beautiful language. ______ some argued that its plot was too slow for modern readers.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For instance,","Similarly,","However,","Therefore,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Raja Alem is a Saudi novelist.\n• She grew up in Makkah.\n• In 2011, she shared the International Prize for Arabic Fiction for \"The Dove's Necklace.\"\n• She was the first woman to win the prize.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize Raja Alem's historic achievement. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Raja Alem grew up in Makkah.","The prize is awarded every year.","Raja Alem is a novelist.","In 2011, the Saudi novelist Raja Alem became the first woman to win the International Prize for Arabic Fiction, sharing the award for \"The Dove's Necklace.\""],"answer":"In 2011, the Saudi novelist Raja Alem became the first woman to win the International Prize for Arabic Fiction, sharing the award for \"The Dove's Necklace.\"","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"having translated into arabic, the readers loved the novel.\"","options":["having been translated into Arabic, the novel was loved by readers.","Having been translated into Arabic, the readers loved the novel.","Having been translated into Arabic, the novel was loved by readers.","Having translated into Arabic, the novel was loved by readers."],"answer":"Having been translated into Arabic, the novel was loved by readers.","explanation":"Implied subject = main subject.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل الضمني."},{"q":"Which sentence contains an absolute construction?","options":["We read inside while it rained.","The rain falling heavily, we stayed inside and read.","We stayed inside because it was raining heavily.","Falling heavily, we stayed inside and read."],"answer":"The rain falling heavily, we stayed inside and read.","explanation":"Absolute vs dangling participle.","explanation_ar":"المطلق والمعلّق."},{"q":"Which tense is normally used to write about the events of a novel?","options":["the past perfect","the future simple","the past continuous","the present simple"],"answer":"the present simple","explanation":"Analytical verb (depict, convey, symbolize…).","explanation_ar":"أفعال التحليل."}],"theme_ar":"الأدب والمجتمع","essential_question_ar":"كيف يعكس الأدب المجتمع، وهل يمكن للقصص أن تغيّر العالم؟","can_do":"I can use absolute constructions (The work completed, …; With the sun setting, …), use advanced participle clauses (Having been translated…, When asked…), discuss literature with precise literary terms, and use dashes, colons and parentheses for stylistic effect.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أستخدم التراكيب المطلقة (The work completed, … / With the sun setting, …)، وأستخدم جمل اسم الفاعل واسم المفعول المتقدمة (Having been translated… / When asked…)، وأناقش الأدب بمصطلحات أدبية دقيقة، وأستخدم الشَّرطة والنقطتين والأقواس لأغراض أسلوبية."});
